    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Evolutionary psychology suggests that human cognitive traits evolved in response to different ancestral environments and survival challenges. For example, in areas with dense forests and a shortage of large game, groups developed group hunting strategies, leading to traits like cooperation and complex communication. High infant mortality rates in some populations could have favored immediate nurturing behaviors, affecting temperament and caregiving practices. Also, the necessity to gather diverse plant resources might have led to better recognition of plant structures, impacting cognitive processing in related tasks today.",
